# Espresso Only: How to Drink Like a Local

If you are in Roman piazza craving a frothy cappuccino after lunch, think twice. In Italy, there’s a famous unwritten rule: **No cappuccino after 11 a.m.**

It’s not a rule written anywhere, but a deeply rooted cultural habit. To Italians, cappuccino is strictly a **breakfast treat**, meant to be paired with a pastry to start the day. The logic is simple: digestion. Italians believe that a large amount of hot milk after a heavy meal is a recipe for a sluggish stomach.

Of course, you will not get banned for ordering a milky coffee at 2 p.m., but it is one of the quickest way to label yourself as a "tourist." Instead, if you want to drink like a local after lunch, go for an **espresso** (*un caffè*) or a **caffè macchiato**—which offers just a tiny "stain" of milk without breaking the social etiquette.

